use std
into meshopt_check.amni

Imports: os, gfx.meshopt and gfx.gltf. A standalone program that checks the meshopt decoder on the CPU and prints one line per check, then "meshopt: ok" when every check passed or "meshopt: FAILED" otherwise. The decoder already exists and must not be defined here: `meshopt::decode(src, count, stride, mode, filter, out)` takes a read-only byte slice, four ints and a growable byte list, appends the decoded bytes to the list and gives a bool. A module bool `failed` starts false; `check(name, ok, detail)` prints "PASS " or "FAIL " then the name, then " (" the detail ")" when the detail is not empty, and sets `failed` when ok is false.

`u32at(b, at)` reads a 32-bit little-endian unsigned number from a byte slice.

The cases file is the first program argument when one is given (`os::arg_count()` above 1, read with `os::arg(1, out)`), otherwise "hc/test/gfx/fixtures/meshopt_cases.bin". When `os::read_file` fails it checks "cases file" as false with the path as detail and stops. The file is a list of records, each: mode, filter, count and stride as 32-bit little-endian numbers, then the stream's length and the stream's bytes, then the expected output's length and the expected bytes. Records are read until the end of the file; a record that runs past the end fails the check "cases file is whole".

M1: every record decodes, the call gives true and the output equals the expected bytes. Count the records that pass and the total; the detail is passed count, " of ", total, and the first failing record's number, mode, filter, count and stride when there is one. Also add up the expected bytes and the time spent inside the decode calls (from `os::clock_ns()`).

M2: the same records split by kind each pass: attribute streams whose first byte is 0xa0, attribute streams whose first byte is 0xa1, attribute records with each filter 1, 2, 3 and 4 (at least one record of each), triangle records and index records. One check line per kind with its passed and total counts; a kind with no records fails.

M3: damaged streams never crash and never give wrong-sized output. For every record whose stream has at least 2 bytes: decode the stream cut short by one byte, then the stream with its first byte changed to 0x55, then a copy with the byte in the middle of the stream bitwise-xored with 0x5a, then a copy with every byte from the middle on set to 0xff. Each call that gives true must have appended exactly count times stride bytes and each call that gives false must have appended nothing. The cut-short and bad-header calls must give false. The detail is the number of damaged decodes tried.

M4: decode speed. The detail is the expected megabytes over the decode seconds from M1, with one decimal, then " MB/s". It passes when that speed is above 70 MB/s.

M5: whole files load the same. These already exist in namespace gltf and must not be defined here: `gltf::reset()` empties the loader; `gltf::load(path)` reads a .glb and gives an asset number, or -1 with the reason from `gltf::error()`; the module lists `gltf::verts` (f32), `gltf::indices` (u32), `gltf::prims` (records with int fields vfirst, vcount, ifirst, icount) and `gltf::anim_keys` when present are the loaded data. A helper `snapshot(path)` resets the loader, loads the file and gives a copy of `gltf::verts`, a copy of `gltf::indices` and the number of prims, or reports the load error. Three files in the same folder as the cases file are compared: "scene.glb" (plain) against "scene_meshopt.glb" (EXT_meshopt_compression, stream version 0) and "scene_meshopt1.glb" (KHR_meshopt_compression, stream version 1): one check line each, passing when both load, the prim counts match and the vertex and index lists are equal value for value. The detail is the vertex float count, the index count and the prim count.

M6: when a second program argument names a plain .glb and a third names its meshopt copy, load each 7 times after a reset, timing every load with `os::clock_ns()`, and print one info line (not a check) with both file sizes in kilobytes, the fastest load of each in milliseconds with one decimal, and whether they match; a mismatch fails the check "big file matches". They match when the vertex lists are equal value for value, the index lists have the same length, and each triangle (indices 3 times t, plus 1, plus 2) of the meshopt copy is the plain triangle or one of its two rotations (b, c, a) or (c, a, b), because the meshopt index encoder may rotate a triangle's corners while keeping its winding.

`main` runs M1, M2, M3 and M4 when the cases file loads, then M5 and M6 always, and finally prints "meshopt: ok" or "meshopt: FAILED".
